WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.140
Actor Todd Lawson LaTourrette's status as
a war veteran and amputee has helped him carve

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.720
out a decent career in Hollywood.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.080
It's a somewhat unconventional angle, but
it worked for LaTourrette, who has scored

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.790
small parts in big films like the George Clooney
war comedy The Men Who Stare at Goats, as

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:20.080
well as roles in series like Netflix's modern
Western crime drama Longmire.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.420
"Got a lot of old regulars up here.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.420
Ah!

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:24.980
Here he is."

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.320
And, most recently, LaTourrette appeared in
the critically acclaimed Better Call Saul.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:33.901
Fans of the Breaking Bad spin-off series will
recognize LaTourrette from the season 4 episode

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:37.240
"Quite a Ride," which aired on AMC on September
3rd.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.640
LaTourrette scored the gig due in part to
his missing appendage, which he previously

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.039
claimed was the unfortunate outcome of an
injury he sustained while serving overseas.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.860
However, it turns out, that was a total lie.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.240
Speaking with Albuquerque NBC-affiliate KOB
4, LaTourrette revealed that he isn't really

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.460
a war veteran, and that he didn't lose his
hand and part of his arm during active duty.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.260
In reality, he cut off and cauterized his
own hand and then lied about his identity

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.300
as a military vet in the hopes of landing
roles in film and on television.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.660
LaTourrette's lies helped him achieve his
goal, but the guilt his fibbing left him with

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.000
far outweighs the benefits he's reaped.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.830
LaTourette explained that the incident took
place in 2001, when he wasn't taking medications

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.000
meant to help him manage his bipolar disorder.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.280
He told the station,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.540
"I severed my own hand with a skill saw.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.800
The state of my mind was a psychotic episode."

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:35.000
He added that Hollywood believed his story
and nobody questioned how he became an amputee,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.780
and he was seen as a unique casting opportunity.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.260
His missing limb and how his character lost
it was even a plot point in this episode of

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.760
The CW's supernatural drama The Messengers:

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.040
"How exactly did you lose it?

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:49.560
Your arm?"

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.020
"I was riding shotgun in a Humvee in Kandahar.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.380
The attention and praise may have felt good
in the moment, but it didn't last forever,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.880
and left LaTourrette feeling guilty.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.120
LaTourette, who is reportedly back on his
bipolar disorder medication as of the making

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:09.579
of this video, noted that he's fully aware
of the consequences and fallout that will

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.840
result from his confession, but felt it was
crucial to come clean.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.640
"I'm ousting myself from the New Mexico Film
Industry.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.680
And gladly so, just to say what I've said."

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.330
He was also candid in stating that he doesn't
expect anyone to forgive him for his wrongdoings,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:28.550
but he remains hopeful that him opening up
about his struggles with mental illness and

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.159
the extremes his bipolar disorder drove him
to will motivate others to seek help if they

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:33.660
need it.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.680
"The power is in your hands to take that medication
in the morning, or at night.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.820
So that, this, this discourse of my life doesn't
need to necessarily be yours."

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.740
If you or someone that you know is struggling
with mental illness, call the Substance Abuse

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:53.520
and Mental Health Services Administration's
national helpline at 1-800-662-HELP, visit

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.780
the National Institute of Mental Health online
at the address on your screen, or call the

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:04.030
National Suicide Prevention Lifeline at 1-800-273-8255.

